# --- Phase 163 (CIGATE-02): SQL brought under the prettier gate ---
# Enabling prettier-plugin-sql makes `yarn format` rewrite every `.sql` file still in scope, so
# the two groups below are named BEFORE the parser exists rather than after it has written.
#
# (a) Byte-exact codemod fixtures. `.claude/skills/ship-review-stack/sources/hygiene-codemod.mjs`
# asserts `changed === expected` — a whole-file string equality — over the four input/expected
# pairs in its `FIXTURES` table, one pair of which is `.sql`, and prettier was measured to rewrite
# BOTH halves of it. That would destroy the transform's only proof. The `.claude/` entry at the
# top of this file (added by 153-05 for the svelte fixtures) already covers them, so this entry is
# knowingly redundant: it states the SQL-specific reason at the artefact, so that narrowing the
# blanket entry cannot silently reopen the trap. The whole directory is named rather than the
# `.sql` extension, because the `.ts`, `.svelte` and `.sh` halves are under the same byte-exactness
# contract and are only prettier-clean today by coincidence. The `.planning/` twins of these
# fixtures are already covered by the `.planning/` entry above.
.claude/skills/ship-review-stack/sources/fixtures/
# (b) Non-source SQL. `apps/supabase/supabase/snippets/` holds one Supabase Studio scratch export
# whose filename ("Untitled query 786.sql", space and all) says it is not source. `.gitignore:53`
# already hides it from prettier, because prettier 3 reads `.gitignore` as an ignore path by
# default — but that line exists to stop Studio exports being committed, not to shape the format
# gate, and one export is committed regardless. Naming it here makes the exclusion survive a change
# to either of those two accidents.
apps/supabase/supabase/snippets/
# There is deliberately NO `apps/supabase/benchmarks/` entry here, and the absence is the finding.
# 163-RESEARCH section P5.5 measured 12 of 12 pgbench scripts failing to parse on 2026-08-28 — their
# psql meta-commands are not SQL — and called that exclusion forced rather than discretionary,
# because a prettier ERROR exits 2, reddens `format:check` permanently, and cannot be repaired by
# `yarn format`. Commit `0c1b876f6 chore(156-09)` then deleted the whole benchmark suite, so there
# is nothing left to exclude and a glob for a path that does not exist would be a dead entry
# carrying a dated measurement about files nobody can find. Restore that suite and the exclusion
# becomes forced again; the symptom is `npx prettier --check "**/*.sql"` exiting 2 rather than 1.
# --- Applied migrations ---
# Every file under `apps/supabase/supabase/migrations/` is an applied migration, and
# `scripts/assert-schema-migration-parity.mjs` compares their bytes. A formatter that rewrote one
# would produce a red gate no edit could repair, so the whole directory is named and a migration
# added to it is out of scope on arrival. The hand-edited sources under
# `apps/supabase/supabase/schema/` stay in scope and are prettier-clean.
apps/supabase/supabase/migrations/
# --- psql scripts ---
# These two files are run with `psql -f` and use its meta-commands (`\set`, `\getenv`, `\if`,
# `\bind`), which are not SQL. prettier-plugin-sql cannot parse them: it stops at the first
# `\set` with a parse error, and a prettier error exits 2, reddens `format:check` and cannot be
# repaired by `yarn format`. Each file is named, not the directory, so that plain SQL added
# beside them stays in scope.
infra/scripts/bootstrap.sql
infra/scripts/check-seed.sql
